What is the treatment of choice for an odontogenic keratocyst, a cystic lesion commonly found in the posterior mandible with a high recurrence rate?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The treatment of choice for an odontogenic keratocyst is $\boxed{\text{enucleation with curettage}}$. This approach is preferred because odontogenic keratocysts have a high recurrence rate, and thorough curettage helps remove any residual cystic lining that could lead to recurrence. The cyst lining is typically 5-8 cells thick with a characteristic parakeratinized layer, making complete removal essential.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

